## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, such a series exists.

**Construction.** Take the specific value to be $x_0 = 0$ and define
$$f_i(x) = i!\, x^i, \qquad i = 0, 1, 2, \dots$$
so the series is $\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{\infty} i!\, x^i$ (with the convention $0^0 = 1$, so $f_0(x) = 1$).

**Convergence at $x = 0$.** At $x = 0$ every term with $i \geq 1$ vanishes, so the series reduces to $f_0(0) = 1$. Hence it converges (trivially) at $x = 0$.

**Divergence at every $x \neq 0$.** Fix any $x \neq 0$ and consider the ratio of consecutive terms:
$$\left|\frac{f_{i+1}(x)}{f_i(x)}\right| = \frac{(i+1)!\,|x|^{i+1}}{i!\,|x|^i} = (i+1)\,|x|.$$
Since $|x| > 0$, we have $(i+1)|x| \to \infty > 1$ as $i \to \infty$. By the **ratio test**, the series $\sum f_i(x)$ diverges. (Equivalently, the general term $i!\,|x|^i \to \infty$, so it fails to tend to $0$, which already forces divergence.)

**Why the radius of convergence is exactly $0$.** Viewing this as a power series $\sum a_i x^i$ with $a_i = i!$, the radius of convergence is
$$R = \frac{1}{\limsup_{i\to\infty} |a_i|^{1/i}} = \frac{1}{\limsup_{i\to\infty} (i!)^{1/i}}.$$
Since $(i!)^{1/i} \to \infty$ (e.g. by Stirling's approximation, $(i!)^{1/i} \sim i/e$), we get $R = 0$. A power series converges absolutely inside its radius of convergence and diverges outside it; with $R = 0$ the only point of convergence is the center $x = 0$.

**Conclusion.** The series $\sum_{i=0}^{\infty} i!\, x^i$ converges at exactly one value of $x$, namely $x = 0$, and diverges for every $x \neq 0$. Therefore such a series exists. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
